{
  "title": "Collagen",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/basic-science/9013/collagen",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Mar 16 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Overview",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Collagen is a naturally occurring family of proteins",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "over 25 types of collagen have been described",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Makes up the **most abundant type of protein** found in humans",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "~25% of the whole-body protein content is composed of some type of collagen",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "found in multiple tissues like cartilage, tendon, bone, ligament, skin (see table below)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "multiple different forms of collagen exist, each with different biomechanical properties suited to the environment in which that form is expressed",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Collagen is made of elongated fibrils **formed by fibroblast cells**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "synthesis of collagen is unique in that it occurs both inside and outside the cell",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Biochemisty",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Composition",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "collagen is composed of a **triple helix** of",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/9013/images/collagen.jpg",
                  "caption": "cartoon of the collagen triple helix formation to a collagen fiber"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "two alpha1 chains",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "one alpha2 chain",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "several common amino acid sequences are found in collagen including",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/9013/images/collagen-alpha-chain.jpg",
                  "caption": "cartoon of the collagen alpha chain made of the amino acids hydroxyproline-proline-glycine"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Glycine-X-Hydroxyproline",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Glycine-Proline-X",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Formation of collagen fibers **alpha chains** three alpha chains (two alpha1 and one alpha 2) are formed **procollagen** two alpha1 and one alpha2 combine to form procollagen **tropocollagen** procollagen is processed by extracellular protein modification into a tropocollagen molecule after being expressed from the golgi apparatus **collagen fibril** aldehyde formation on tropocollagen lysine and hydroxylysine allow for covalent bonding between tropocollagen molecules forming the collagen fibril **collagen fiber** multiple collagen fibrils aggregate to form a collagen fiber",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/9013/images/collagen%20processing.jpg",
              "caption": "Illustration showing the processing and formation of a collagen fiber"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
